    "q-114115": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114115",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(q)=24{q}^{5}-62{q}^{4}+14{q}^{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(q)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n24{q}^{5}-62{q}^{4}+14{q}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2{q}^{3}.$ We can factor out the $2{q}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{q}^{3}(12{q}^{2}-31q+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $12{q}^{2}-31q+7$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{q}^{3}(12{q}^{2}-31q+7) & =0 \\\\ 2{q}^{3}(4q-1)(3q-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $q=0,$ $q=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $q=\\frac{7}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
